<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osnabrück</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osnabrück is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Hase river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> founded a bishopric there around 780, and its prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled a territory in north-west Germany until 1803. Together with Münster it hosted the negotiations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> in 1648.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osnabruck.jpg" alt="Osnabrück" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: El Grafo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osnabr%C3%BCck_aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Osnabrück lies on the river Hase in a valley between the Wiehen Hills and the northern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. Although it belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, it is historically, culturally and linguistically considered part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. The origin of its name is disputed, and the city gave its name to the textile fabric osnaburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Osnabrück began as a marketplace beside the bishopric founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in 780. Some time before 803 it became the seat of the Prince-Bishopric of Osnabrück, probably the oldest bishopric in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>. It was first called a city in 1147, and a decade later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> granted it fortification rights. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in the 12th century. In 1582, under Mayor Hammacher, 163 women were executed as alleged witches, most of them burned alive, and 276 women and two men were executed in all between 1561 and 1639, a period of unrest that also saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><p>The first Lutheran services were held in 1543, but the Catholic churches continued to operate and the city never became wholly Lutheran. A Catholic bishop was elected in 1623, and troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> occupied the city in 1628. The Gymnasium Carolinum became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> university in 1632, which closed a year later when Swedish troops took the city. Peace negotiations were held in Osnabrück and Münster from 1643 to 1648, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> recognised Osnabrück as bi-confessional: the prince-bishopric was to be held alternately by a Catholic and a Lutheran bishop, the Protestant chosen from the descendants of the Dukes of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>.</p><p>The Napoleonic period saw the city change hands several times. It passed to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1803, briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1806, was in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> from 1807 to 1810, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">First French Empire</a>, and after 1815 in the Kingdom of Hanover. Prussia annexed it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866. In 1930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> won nearly 28 per cent of the vote in the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani people</a> were deported during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. British troops entered the city on 4 April 1945 with little resistance, after extensive bombing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old town was reconstructed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in designs that follow the original medieval architecture, and most of the towers of the old fortifications are still visible. Osnabrück was the home of the largest British garrison outside the United Kingdom. It has a university and a university of applied sciences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osnabrück” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Treaties signed at Münster and <b>Osnabrück</b> ended the Thirty Years' War, which had devastated central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-24">24 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Diepholz are first recorded around 1085 as noble lords on the Hunte river, between <b>Osnabrück</b> and Bremen.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-diepholz">County of Diepholz</a></li><li><blockquote>They were advocates of the bishoprics of Münster and <b>Osnabrück</b>, which gave them influence and frequent conflicts with the bishops.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tecklenburg">County of Tecklenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne founded the diocese of <b>Osnabrück</b> around 780 after his conquest of the Saxons.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-osnabruck">Prince-Bishopric of Osnabrück</a></li></ul></section>
